Chapter: Occurrence
  cites Codex chapter Build Settings (max-recursion-depth)

 The occurrence analyser: the highest-leverage pre-pass in the GHC
 simplifier architecture (MiddleEndLiterature.md section 2). For a
 definition it answers, per binder in that definition, how many times
 the binder is referenced within its scope — Dead (0), Once, or Many
 (two or more). That single fact is what a GHC-style simplifier
 (WS-D3, not this unit) consumes to make two rewrites free and safe:
 drop dead let-bindings — DCE stops being a pass — and inline an
 occurs-once binding unconditionally, because inlining a value used
 exactly once neither duplicates work nor grows code.

 This unit is an ANALYSIS. It reads IRExpr and returns a result; it
 rewrites nothing and it is not wired into the pipeline that produces
 binaries. It is dead code in the shipping compiler — parsed and
 type-checked with the rest of the tree, but unreachable from
 `opening`, so codegen tree-shakes it and the self-compile is
 byte-identical to the seed. It links into WS-D3 when the simplifier
 that consumes it lands, exactly as the interval kernel waited for its
 consumer.

 The count is computed in ONE pass, not per-name. Counting one name at
 a time over a tree is quadratic in the number of binders; instead each
 subtree returns the MAP from every free name to its OccCount, and a
 parent combines its children's maps. Union SUMS counts: a name free in
 two siblings is used at least twice, so it is Many. This is
 deliberately conservative at joins — the two arms of an IrIf are
 alternatives, not both taken, yet their maps are summed, so a name
 used once in each arm classifies Many. That is the SAFE direction for
 the inliner it feeds: inlining an occurs-once binding into both arms
 of a case duplicates the bound work down every path, which occurs-once
 inlining must never do. Over-counting to Many forbids exactly that;
 under-counting to Once would license it. So the join sums.

 Scope removal is the crux. At every binder — the name of an IrLet, the
 parameters of an IrLambda, the variables of a match pattern, the
 binder of an IrDoBind — the name's occurrences in the body are what is
 reported FOR THAT BINDER, and the name is REMOVED from the map that
 propagates upward, because it is not free above its binder. Shadowing
 falls out for free: removing the name at the inner binder consumes the
 inner occurrences; the outer binder of the same name sees only what is
 left, which is why an inner and an outer binding of one name are
 classified independently. The tree is correctly nested (the 2.22 bug
 was the emitter's flat table, never the tree), so lexical scope is
 just structural recursion.

 An IrDoBind scopes over the statements AFTER it, not the whole block:
 a bind's classification is read from the free map of the rest of the
 sequence, and the name is removed from that map before it rises past
 the bind. The do-notation the 2.22 lowering fix made real is a
 sequence in which each bind scopes over the tail.

 The walk is depth-bounded by the compiler's shared recursion budget,
 so a pathological IR is reported as unwalked rather than hung — the
 analysis never answers about a subtree it did not read.

 Limitations, stated so they are not assumed away: the join is
 conservative (once-per-arm reads as Many), which is sound for the
 inliner but not tight for a size heuristic that later wants the true
 per-arm count; loop-breaker selection for recursive binding groups
 (the other half of GHC's occurrence analyser) is not done here, because
 this IR has no recursive let groups — recursion is between top-level
 definitions, which this per-definition analysis does not cross; and the
 map is an association list scanned linearly, chosen for the binder
 counts a single definition carries, not for a definition with thousands
 of distinct free names.

Section: Occurrence Count

 Dead is absence: a name with no free occurrence is never stored in a
 map, and a lookup that misses answers Dead. occ-bump records one more
 occurrence — the only way a count ever rises — and saturates at Many,
 because the classification cares only about zero, one, and many.

  OccCount =
   | OccDead
   | OccOnce
   | OccMany

  occ-bump : OccCount -> OccCount
  occ-bump (c) =
   when c
    is OccDead -> OccOnce
    is OccOnce -> OccMany
    is OccMany -> OccMany

  occ-count-name : OccCount -> Text
  occ-count-name (c) =
   when c
    is OccDead -> "Dead"
    is OccOnce -> "Once"
    is OccMany -> "Many"

Section: Occurrence Map

 A map from free name to its count, and the walk's result: the free map
 of a subtree together with the classifications collected for every
 binder seen inside it. The binder list threads through the walk as an
 accumulator so its order is the traversal order — a subtree's binders
 before the binder that scopes them — and is deterministic.

  OccEntry = record {
   name : Text,
   count : OccCount
  }

  OccResult = record {
   free : List OccEntry,
   binders : List OccEntry
  }

  occ-map-find : List OccEntry, Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  occ-map-find (m) (n) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then 0 - 1
   else if (list-at m i).name == n then i
   else occ-map-find m n (i + 1) len

  occ-map-get : List OccEntry, Text -> OccCount
  occ-map-get (m) (n) =
   let i = occ-map-find m n 0 (list-length m)
   in if i < 0 then OccDead else (list-at m i).count

 Recording a name that is already present bumps it — Once becomes Many,
 Many stays Many — which is why any collision in a union lands on Many:
 both sides already carry at least one occurrence. A name not yet
 present is stored with the count the other side brought, so a subtree
 that already reached Many keeps it.

  occ-insert : List OccEntry, Text, OccCount -> List OccEntry
  occ-insert (m) (n) (c) =
   let i = occ-map-find m n 0 (list-length m)
   in if i < 0 then list-push m (OccEntry { name = n, count = c })
   else list-set-at m i (OccEntry { name = n, count = occ-bump ((list-at m i).count) })

  occ-singleton : Text -> List OccEntry
  occ-singleton (n) = occ-insert [] n (occ-bump OccDead)

  occ-union : List OccEntry, List OccEntry -> List OccEntry
  occ-union (m1) (m2) = occ-union-loop m1 m2 0 (list-length m2)

  occ-union-loop : List OccEntry, List OccEntry, Integer, Integer -> List OccEntry
  occ-union-loop (acc) (m2) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else let e = list-at m2 i
   in occ-union-loop (occ-insert acc (e.name) (e.count)) m2 (i + 1) len

  occ-remove : List OccEntry, Text -> List OccEntry
  occ-remove (m) (n) = occ-remove-loop [] m n 0 (list-length m)

  occ-remove-loop : List OccEntry, List OccEntry, Text, Integer, Integer -> List OccEntry
  occ-remove-loop (acc) (m) (n) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else let e = list-at m i
   in occ-remove-loop (if (e.name) == n then acc else list-push acc e) m n (i + 1) len

 Binding a name: its classification is read from the free map at the
 binder, the entry is appended to the binder list, and the name is
 stripped from the free map that rises above the binder.

  occ-bind-one : Text, List OccEntry, List OccEntry -> OccResult
  occ-bind-one (n) (free) (binders) =
   OccResult {
    free = occ-remove free n,
    binders = list-push binders (OccEntry { name = n, count = occ-map-get free n })
   }

Section: Expression Walk

 One pass over the tree. Each node returns the free map of its subtree
 and the binder list accumulated through it; the accumulator threads
 left to right so a parent hands each child the binders its earlier
 children produced.

  occ-expr : IRExpr, List OccEntry, Integer -> OccResult
  occ-expr (e) (acc) (depth) =
   if depth >= max-recursion-depth then OccResult { free = [], binders = acc }
   else when e
    is IrIntLit (v) (s) | IrNumLit (v) (s) | IrTextLit (v) (s) | IrBoolLit (v) (s) | IrCharLit (v) (s) -> OccResult { free = [], binders = acc }
    is IrName (n) (t) (s) -> OccResult { free = occ-singleton n, binders = acc }
    is IrNegate (x) (s) -> occ-expr x acc (depth + 1)
    is IrBinary (op) (l) (r) (t) (s) ->
     let rl = occ-expr l acc (depth + 1)
     in let rr = occ-expr r (rl.binders) (depth + 1)
     in OccResult { free = occ-union (rl.free) (rr.free), binders = rr.binders }
    is IrIf (c) (th) (el) (t) (s) ->
     let rc = occ-expr c acc (depth + 1)
     in let rt = occ-expr th (rc.binders) (depth + 1)
     in let re = occ-expr el (rt.binders) (depth + 1)
     in OccResult { free = occ-union (rc.free) (occ-union (rt.free) (re.free)), binders = re.binders }
    is IrLet (n) (t) (v) (b) (s) ->
     let rv = occ-expr v acc (depth + 1)
     in let rb = occ-expr b (rv.binders) (depth + 1)
     in OccResult {
      free = occ-union (rv.free) (occ-remove (rb.free) n),
      binders = list-push (rb.binders) (OccEntry { name = n, count = occ-map-get (rb.free) n })
     }
    is IrApply (f) (a) (t) (s) ->
     let rf = occ-expr f acc (depth + 1)
     in let ra = occ-expr a (rf.binders) (depth + 1)
     in OccResult { free = occ-union (rf.free) (ra.free), binders = ra.binders }
    is IrLambda (ps) (b) (t) (s) ->
     let rb = occ-expr b acc (depth + 1)
     in occ-bind-params ps (rb.free) (rb.binders) 0 (list-length ps)
    is IrList (es) (t) (s) -> occ-exprs es acc (depth + 1) 0 (list-length es) []
    is IrMatch (sc) (bs) (t) (s) ->
     let rsc = occ-expr sc acc (depth + 1)
     in occ-branches bs (rsc.free) (rsc.binders) (depth + 1) 0 (list-length bs)
    is IrAct (ss) (t) (s) -> occ-stmts ss acc (depth + 1) 0 (list-length ss)
    is IrHandle (eff) (h) (cs) (t) (s) ->
     let rh = occ-expr h acc (depth + 1)
     in occ-clauses cs (rh.free) (rh.binders) (depth + 1) 0 (list-length cs)
    is IrWithTimeout (secs) (effs) (scopes) (body) (t) (s) -> occ-expr body acc (depth + 1)
    is IrRecord (n) (fs) (t) (s) -> occ-fields fs acc (depth + 1) 0 (list-length fs) []
    is IrFieldAccess (r) (f) (t) (s) -> occ-expr r acc (depth + 1)
    is IrFork (b) (t) (s) -> occ-expr b acc (depth + 1)
    is IrAwait (tk) (t) (s) -> occ-expr tk acc (depth + 1)
    is IrTry (mx) (body) (fb) (fail) (t) (s) ->
     let r1 = occ-stmts body acc (depth + 1) 0 (list-length body)
     in let r2 = occ-stmts fb (r1.binders) (depth + 1) 0 (list-length fb)
     in let r3 = occ-stmts fail (r2.binders) (depth + 1) 0 (list-length fail)
     in OccResult { free = occ-union (r1.free) (occ-union (r2.free) (r3.free)), binders = r3.binders }
    is IrFieldStore (r) (f) (v) (t) (s) ->
     let rr = occ-expr r acc (depth + 1)
     in let rv = occ-expr v (rr.binders) (depth + 1)
     in OccResult { free = occ-union (rr.free) (rv.free), binders = rv.binders }
    is IrError (m) (t) (s) -> OccResult { free = [], binders = acc }

  occ-exprs : List IRExpr, List OccEntry, Integer, Integer, Integer, List OccEntry -> OccResult
  occ-exprs (es) (acc) (depth) (i) (len) (freeacc) =
   if i >= len then OccResult { free = freeacc, binders = acc }
   else let r = occ-expr (list-at es i) acc depth
   in occ-exprs es (r.binders) depth (i + 1) len (occ-union freeacc (r.free))

  occ-fields : List IRFieldVal, List OccEntry, Integer, Integer, Integer, List OccEntry -> OccResult
  occ-fields (fs) (acc) (depth) (i) (len) (freeacc) =
   if i >= len then OccResult { free = freeacc, binders = acc }
   else let f = list-at fs i
   in let r = occ-expr (f.value) acc depth
   in occ-fields fs (r.binders) depth (i + 1) len (occ-union freeacc (r.free))

 A lambda's parameters are binders over its body: each is classified
 against the body's free map and stripped from it.

  occ-bind-params : List IRParam, List OccEntry, List OccEntry, Integer, Integer -> OccResult
  occ-bind-params (ps) (free) (binders) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then OccResult { free = free, binders = binders }
   else let p = list-at ps i
   in let r = occ-bind-one (p.name) free binders
   in occ-bind-params ps (r.free) (r.binders) (i + 1) len

 An act statement's binder is in scope for the statements after it. The
 rest of the sequence is analysed first; a bind reads its classification
 from that tail's free map and removes its name before the map rises.

  occ-stmts : List IRActStmt, List OccEntry, Integer, Integer, Integer -> OccResult
  occ-stmts (ss) (acc) (depth) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then OccResult { free = [], binders = acc }
   else let s = list-at ss i
   in when s
    is IrDoExec (v) (sp) ->
     let rv = occ-expr v acc depth
     in let rrest = occ-stmts ss (rv.binders) depth (i + 1) len
     in OccResult { free = occ-union (rv.free) (rrest.free), binders = rrest.binders }
    is IrDoBind (n) (t) (v) (sp) ->
     let rv = occ-expr v acc depth
     in let rrest = occ-stmts ss (rv.binders) depth (i + 1) len
     in OccResult {
      free = occ-union (rv.free) (occ-remove (rrest.free) n),
      binders = list-push (rrest.binders) (OccEntry { name = n, count = occ-map-get (rrest.free) n })
     }

 A branch's pattern binds over its guard and body only. Guard and body
 are analysed, their maps unioned, and each pattern variable classified
 and stripped; the surviving map joins the scrutinee's above.

  occ-branches : List IRBranch, List OccEntry, List OccEntry, Integer, Integer, Integer -> OccResult
  occ-branches (bs) (free) (binders) (depth) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then OccResult { free = free, binders = binders }
   else let b = list-at bs i
   in let rg = occ-expr (b.guard) binders depth
   in let rbody = occ-expr (b.body) (rg.binders) depth
   in let branchfree = occ-union (rg.free) (rbody.free)
   in let pr = occ-bind-pat branchfree (b.pattern) (rbody.binders) depth
   in occ-branches bs (occ-union free (pr.free)) (pr.binders) depth (i + 1) len

  occ-bind-pat : List OccEntry, IRPat, List OccEntry, Integer -> OccResult
  occ-bind-pat (free) (p) (binders) (depth) =
   if depth >= max-recursion-depth then OccResult { free = free, binders = binders }
   else when p
    is IrVarPat (n) (t) (s) -> occ-bind-one n free binders
    is IrCtorPat (n) (subs) (t) (s) -> occ-bind-pat-list free subs binders (depth + 1) 0 (list-length subs)
    is IrVecPat (subs) (t) (s) -> occ-bind-pat-list free subs binders (depth + 1) 0 (list-length subs)
    is otherwise -> OccResult { free = free, binders = binders }

  occ-bind-pat-list : List OccEntry, List IRPat, List OccEntry, Integer, Integer, Integer -> OccResult
  occ-bind-pat-list (free) (ps) (binders) (depth) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then OccResult { free = free, binders = binders }
   else let r = occ-bind-pat free (list-at ps i) binders depth
   in occ-bind-pat-list (r.free) ps (r.binders) depth (i + 1) len

 A handle clause binds its operation parameters and resume name over
 its body.

  occ-clauses : List IRHandleClause, List OccEntry, List OccEntry, Integer, Integer, Integer -> OccResult
  occ-clauses (cs) (free) (binders) (depth) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then OccResult { free = free, binders = binders }
   else let c = list-at cs i
   in let rb = occ-expr (c.body) binders depth
   in let r1 = occ-bind-names (rb.free) (c.params) (rb.binders) 0 (list-length (c.params))
   in let r2 = occ-bind-one (c.resume-name) (r1.free) (r1.binders)
   in occ-clauses cs (occ-union free (r2.free)) (r2.binders) depth (i + 1) len

  occ-bind-names : List OccEntry, List Text, List OccEntry, Integer, Integer -> OccResult
  occ-bind-names (free) (ns) (binders) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then OccResult { free = free, binders = binders }
   else let r = occ-bind-one (list-at ns i) free binders
   in occ-bind-names (r.free) ns (r.binders) (i + 1) len

Section: Definition Analysis

 The per-definition result the WS-D3 simplifier consumes: one entry per
 binder in the definition, parameters first in declaration order, then
 the internal binders in traversal order. A parameter's classification
 is read from the free map of the whole body.

  occ-analyse-def : IRDef -> List OccEntry
  occ-analyse-def (d) =
   let r = occ-expr (d.body) [] 0
   in (occ-classify-params (d.params) (r.free) 0 (list-length (d.params)) []) & (r.binders)

  occ-classify-params : List IRParam, List OccEntry, Integer, Integer, List OccEntry -> List OccEntry
  occ-classify-params (ps) (free) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else let p = list-at ps i
   in occ-classify-params ps free (i + 1) len (list-push acc (OccEntry { name = p.name, count = occ-map-get free (p.name) }))

 A rendering of the analysis for a whole chapter, one line per
 definition as `OCC defname: name=Class, name=Class`. The `occ-report`
 pass in Passes.codex returns these lines through the same out-of-band
 violation channel `ir-check` uses, so `-Passes occ-report` dumps the
 analysis of every definition to the compile log without rewriting a
 single node.

  occ-report-chapter : IRChapter -> List Text
  occ-report-chapter (ch) = occ-report-defs (ch.defs) 0 (list-length (ch.defs)) []

  occ-report-defs : List IRDef, Integer, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  occ-report-defs (defs) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else let d = list-at defs i
   in let es = occ-analyse-def d
   in occ-report-defs defs (i + 1) len (list-push acc ("OCC " & (d.name) & ": " & occ-join es 0 (list-length es) ""))

  occ-join : List OccEntry, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  occ-join (es) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else let e = list-at es i
   in occ-join es (i + 1) len (acc & (if acc == "" then "" else ", ") & (e.name) & "=" & occ-count-name (e.count))

 The occurrence report is INFORMATION about well-formed IR, not a
 fault, so it carries an info-severity code of its own. It must not be
 `ir-check`'s CDX9004, which means malformed IR: sharing that code
 would make a real IR corruption and an occurrence line indistinguishable
 to anyone grepping for the former. CDX4030 sits in the info band beside
 CDX4010 (bounds proven) and CDX4020 (proof erased); info diagnostics do
 not set the error flag, so `occ-report` reports and the compile still
 emits its binary.

  cdx-occ-report : Integer = 4030

  occ-info-bag : List Text -> DiagnosticBag
  occ-info-bag (lines) = occ-info-bag-loop empty-bag lines 0 (list-length lines)

  occ-info-bag-loop : DiagnosticBag, List Text, Integer, Integer -> DiagnosticBag
  occ-info-bag-loop (bag) (lines) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then bag
   else occ-info-bag-loop (bag-add bag (make-info cdx-occ-report (list-at lines i) synthetic-span)) lines (i + 1) len

